<VI _name="lvai_create_accessors.vi" description="Create Read and/or Write accessor VIs for every private data field of a LabVIEW class, by calling the LVClass project provider's own wizard body MemberVICreation.lvlib:CLSUIP_CreateNewAccessor.vi once per field, and save each one immediately while its reference is still held. The class is found by PATH among the classes of the project that is active in the IDE. Every input is a string because a helper's controls are set as strings over VI Server; vi type, access ui and the two flags are converted on the diagram. The library is saved ONCE PER FIELD rather than once at the end, so an interrupted run leaves a consistent partial class instead of orphan VIs no class file mentions - measured twice, where a client timeout left 12 of 14 accessor VIs on disk and 0 registered. from field and field count bound the run to a slice of the private data cluster via Array Subset, so a class with many fields can be built in several calls that each fit inside a client's patience; take fields defaults to 2, measured rather than chosen: the per-field save gets slower as the class grows because Save All This Library checks the whole library each time, so the cost per field went 11.5 s empty, about 20 s at 6 members and past 30 s at 12 - three fields fit only the first call. A larger number is still harmless in itself - Array Subset clamps a length past the end - it just may not come back. It is called take fields and not field count because a CONTROL and an INDICATOR may not share a name: with both called field count, Ctrl Val.Set reached the indicator, the control kept its default and the slice was silently ignored - a run asked for 4 fields and built 6. The field count indicator reports the WHOLE cluster so a caller can work out how many calls are still needed; accessorsCreated says what this call did. Saving each accessor inside the loop is deliberate too: the wizard leaves a new VI in memory with no path, and once its reference is gone Save All This Library returns error 43 and the project Save returns 1019, because LabVIEW wants to prompt for a path and cannot. Three references are closed at the end, in order: the LIBRARY reference that Save All This Library hands back - spotted dangling by the user, and the last use of the class reference that CLSUIP_GetAllClassesInProject opened - then the IDE application reference, then the project reference. The reference out terminals on the property nodes are deliberately left unclosed: those are pass-throughs of the same refnum for dataflow ordering, not new references. Leaving the project and application references open was once the leading suspect for LabVIEW leaving a few minutes after a run - 15 DestroyPlatformEvent failures and 14 minidumps in one session log, and NI's own helper for that route is called Open project application ref.vi - so they are closed here whether or not that was ever the cause.">
